  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PROTECTING EAST ANDEAN SUBTROPICAL FORES</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Habitat loss resulting from the creation of cattle pastures and climate change in the Neotropics are key threats to migratory birds. Deforestation is higher in southern Ecuador than elsewhere in South America. This project addresses these crucial threats on an area of 185,560 acres within southeastern Ecuador. This region is a key overwintering site for Cerulean Warbler, Canada Warbler and Olive-sided Flycatcher. In this area, Fundacin Jocotoco owns and protects Copalinga and Tapichalaca Reserves (9,587 acres) and co-manages the Palanda Municipal Reserve (172,973 acres). In the second phase of this project they will expand Tapichalaca with 1,419 acres, increase protection and patrolling of Tapichalaca and Copalinga Reserves, monitor Neotropical migratory bird species in restored and forested transects, continue strengthening the management of the Palanda Municipal Reserve, conduct community outreach with local cattle ranching associations, and expand reforestation efforts in three key watersheds.</narrative>
  </description>
